.TH VYT 1 "2026" "vyt 1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vyt \- a tiny C wrapper around yt-dlp and mpv
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vyt
.B \-m
.I URL
.br
.B vyt
.B \-v
.I URL
.br
.B vyt
.B \-s
.I QUERY
.br
.B vyt
.B playlist
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.br
.B vyt
.B \-V
.br
.B vyt
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vyt
is a tiny wrapper around
.BR yt-dlp (1)
and
.BR mpv (1)
that lets you play or search YouTube content with short,
memorable commands instead of typing out long
.B mpv
or
.B yt-dlp
invocations.
.PP
In addition to single-URL playback and search,
.B vyt
manages persistent named playlists stored as plain text files on disk.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " URL"
Play
.I URL
as music: audio only, via
.BR "mpv --no-video" .
.TP
.BI \-v " URL"
Play
.I URL
as video, via
.BR mpv .
.TP
.BI \-s " QUERY"
Search YouTube for
.I QUERY
via
.BR yt-dlp ,
returning the top 10 results.  On a terminal the results are printed as
a numbered list: a header naming the query and result count, one entry
per result with the title, the duration and channel when yt-dlp
provides them, and the URL on its own line, followed by a reminder of
how to play a result.  When stdout is not a terminal, results are
printed one per line as
.RB ` "title | URL" '
for scripting; see
.B OUTPUT
below.  A search that returns no results is not an error: vyt prints a
note and exits 0.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Show usage and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
.B vyt
aims for output where every line has a purpose.  All messages share one
shape:
.PP
.RS
.nf
vyt: ✓ created playlist 'chill'    success (green check mark)
vyt: no playlists yet                 neutral status
vyt: error: no playlist named 'nope'  failure (red label)
vyt: warning: skipping entry '...'    recoverable problem (yellow label)
vyt: hint: run 'vyt playlist list'    suggested next step (cyan label)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Errors name what failed and, when the cause is known, why.  Most errors
are followed by a
.B vyt: hint:
line containing a copy-pasteable command for the obvious next step.
Diagnostics (errors, warnings, hints, and notes about empty results or
empty playlists) are written to stderr; successful command output
(search results, listings, success confirmations, playback status) is
written to stdout.
.SS Colors
Colors are used only when the target stream is an interactive terminal.
The environment can override this, in order of precedence:
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
A non-empty value disables color entirely (beats everything below).
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
A non-empty value other than
.B 0
forces color on, even for redirected output.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR=0
Disables color.
.TP
.B TERM=dumb
Disables color.
.PP
With color disabled the wording and layout are unchanged; only the
escape sequences are omitted, so output stays readable in monochrome
terminals and logs.
.SS Scriptable output
When stdout is not a terminal, listing commands fall back to stable
plain formats so shell scripts keep working unchanged:
.TP
.B "vyt \-s QUERY"
One
.RB ` "title | URL" '
line per result, no header, no footer.
.TP
.B "vyt playlist list"
One playlist name per line, nothing else.
.TP
.B "vyt playlist song list"
The storage format: one
.RB ` "song_name = url" '
line per song; legacy entries print as just the URL.
.PP
Whether stdout is a terminal affects only the format of these listings,
never their content, stream choice, or exit status.  Long titles and
song names are truncated to the terminal width at a UTF-8 character
boundary; URLs are never truncated, so they remain copy-pasteable.
.SH PLAYLISTS
.B vyt
stores playlists as plain text files under the playlists directory (see
.B FILES
below). Each playlist is one file, each song is one line of the form
.RB ` "song_name = url" ,
and the URL portion must be
.B http://
or
.B https://.
The directory is created on first use.
.SS Playlist commands
.TP
.BI "vyt playlist add " name
Create a new, empty playlist called
.IR name .
Fails if a playlist with that name already exists.
.TP
.B "vyt playlist list"
Print every playlist, sorted alphabetically.  On a terminal each name
is followed by its song count (or
.RB ` empty ');
when stdout is not a terminal only the names are printed.  An empty
playlists directory is not an error: vyt prints a note and a hint to
stderr and exits 0.
.TP
.BI "vyt playlist remove " name
Delete the playlist called
.IR name .
The file is removed; songs are not recoverable.
.TP
.BI "vyt playlist song add " "playlist song_name url"
Append
.I url
to playlist
.I playlist
with the user-provided
.I song_name
as the displayed title.
Duplicates (by URL) are rejected; the URL remains the canonical
identifier for the song.
All of
.IR playlist ,
.IR song_name ,
and
.I url
are validated; see
.B FILES
for the file naming rules and
.B "SONG NAMES"
for the song name rules.
.TP
.BI "vyt playlist song remove " "playlist url"
Remove the first occurrence of
.I url
from playlist
.IR playlist .
The match is by URL, not by name, so a single song can be removed
without ambiguity even if other songs share the same name.
It is not an error if the URL appears more than once; only the first
match is removed.
.TP
.BI "vyt playlist song list " playlist
Print every song in playlist
.IR playlist .
On a terminal the songs are numbered, with each song name on its own
line and its URL dimmed on the line beneath, so long names and long
URLs stay readable; the header names the playlist and the song count.
Songs without a name (legacy entries from older versions of
.BR vyt )
are printed as just the URL.  When stdout is not a terminal the storage
format is printed instead (see
.B OUTPUT
above).
.TP
.BI "vyt playlist play " name
Play playlist
.I name
in random order, using
.B "mpv --no-video"
(the same path as
.BR "vyt \-m" ).
When more than one song is present,
.B vyt
never picks the same song twice in a row.
The "now playing" line shows the song's position in the playlist as
.RB ` "[ position/count] " '
followed by the song name if present, otherwise the URL.
Playback continues indefinitely until interrupted; see
.B SIGNALS
below.
.SS Song names
A song name must be between 1 and 256 bytes, must contain at least one
non-whitespace character, and must not contain control characters. A
song name with leading or trailing whitespace is accepted (the storage
format is whitespace-aware); one made only of whitespace is rejected.
Song names are stored verbatim alongside the URL; the URL remains the
canonical identifier used for duplicate detection, removal, and
playback.
.TP
Writes are atomic: a new playlist is written to a hidden temporary file
in the same directory,
.BR fsync (2)ed,
and
.BR rename (2)ed
over the original.
An interrupted
.B vyt
can never leave a half-written playlist file.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT
During
.BR "vyt playlist play" ,
Ctrl+C stops playback cleanly:
.B vyt
finishes waiting for the currently running
.B mpv
child to exit, prints
.RB ` "vyt: stopped." ',
and exits with status 0.
The signal handler is installed only for the duration of the
.B play
command and restored afterwards.
.TP
.B SIGTERM
Treated identically to
.B SIGINT
during playback.
.SH EXIT STATUS
If
.B vyt
launches
.B mpv
or
.BR yt-dlp ,
its exit status is that of the underlying process.
.PP
For playlist commands,
.B vyt
exits 0 on success and 1 on any error (missing playlist, duplicate URL,
invalid playlist name, invalid song name, malformed URL, filesystem
failure, etc.).
.PP
A search that returns no results exits 0 with a note on stderr.
.PP
Stopping playback with Ctrl+C is considered normal termination, not an
error: the exit status is 0.
.PP
Non-zero if
.B vyt
itself failed to parse arguments or launch the process.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If set and absolute, playlists live under
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vyt/playlists/ .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Non-empty: never emit ANSI color escapes.  See
.B OUTPUT
above for the full precedence rules.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
Non-empty and not "0": emit ANSI escapes even when the output is not a
terminal.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR
Setting it to "0" disables color.
.TP
.B TERM
A value of
.B dumb
disables color.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Consulted for the terminal width when the width cannot be measured
directly.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B yt-dlp
and
.B mpv
must be installed and available on
.BR PATH .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yt-dlp (1),
.BR mpv (1)
